obs AND I MADE THE FAULT MY OWN LANE EXISTS TO CATCH. To stop my own --hot runner before the rebase I typed pkill -TERM -f 'standing_equipment_run.sh --hot'. pkill -f matches a command line rather than a directory, so it reached the whole process table: five PIDs on a pier eight ships share, and the shell exited 144 because the pattern matched my own bash. I then read every peer's standing-equipment-run.lock.d holder out of /proc and all five stand alive, so no peer's roster pass died; two terminated PIDs stay unattributable, since a process that is gone leaves no cwd to read. Booked as its own row. tools/f/fleet_call.sh is the bounded form the tree already owns, and I reached past it.
obs THE PIN HAD NO ROOM FOR TWO ROWS AND I FOLDED A PEER'S BOOKED ONE. %558 stood BOOKED at 3,246 bytes, which is exactly what BOOKED licenses -- reds_fold.sh refuses an OPEN row by name, so the unsafe case cannot be typed. I wrote its shelf head from its own headline rather than characterising a finding I did not measure. That is the second fold this lap: the first, of %550, collided with a peer's fold of the same row landing upstream while I worked, and I withdrew mine whole at the rebase.
obs ERRATUM, WRITTEN BEFORE THE SEND CLOSED. The obs below says my row is %564. Four more rebases followed, upstream spent 564, 565, 566 and 567 while this lap ran, and the two rows landed at %568 and %569 -- five renumberings in one send, every stamp holding. That is the derived spine costing exactly what its law says it costs, and it is why rule 4 asks a lap to cite the stamp until the spine binds the number. The card cites stamps for this reason; unshared_citation caught me citing numbers there and I repaired it before the push.
obs THE REBASE CORRECTED ME TWICE, AND BOTH CORRECTIONS WERE THE SPINE WORKING. %563 was spent upstream while I wrote, so my row is %564 -- the stamp held and the view moved. And upstream had already repaired dated_path, narrowing refs_lost and its ceiling from 175/165 to 85/85 within the hour of my sing. That does not weaken the finding; it is the finding. These are ordinary, reachable reds, and they stood only because nothing asked.
loom pin=construction/REDS.md before=41401 after=38301 bound=40960 rows_folded=1 rows_added=2
file construction/REDS.md two rows booked -- the cadence tier with no caller, and my own pkill -f reaching past its tree; %558 folded out through reds_fold.sh for the room
file construction/archive/REDS-the-roster-that-named-three-rooms-rows-558.md the shelf that BOOKED row folded to, its head quoting the row's own headline rather than restating a peer's finding
obs THE CLOSING PASS IS THE FINDING, STATED BY THE TREE ITSELF. 172 lap guards, 168 green, and the one red is standing_equipment -- red because the five cadence refusals are now RECORDED on the run card rather than silent. cadence_never_run_here fell 58 -> 2, runs_recorded rose 168 -> 228, and the roster's verdict went from ok to roster_broken without a single line of tree behaviour changing. It was green for ten days over a tree holding five red guards. A roster that reads broken the day it is finally asked is the honest reading, and it is not a regression I introduced; it is the silence becoming audible.
obs I SHIP WITH THAT RED STANDING AND SAY SO. Four lanes own the five, none of them mine, and closing another lane's guard inside an audit lap would be the same overreach my second row books. The receipt is withheld while they stand, so the next lap on any ship pays a full cold pass -- which is a real cost, named here rather than hidden, and the strongest argument for whichever answer Keaton gives about where the cadence clock lives.
